Hide the idle notifier from a sandbox

niri hides privileged globals from a client connected through a
wp-security-context-v1 socket, but ext_idle_notifier_v1 was still
offered to it, so a program in a sandbox could learn the moment the
person stopped touching the machine and act while nobody watched.
smithay's IdleNotifierState takes no client filter, unlike the other
states niri restricts.

So niri dispatches the idle notifier's global itself, as
delegate_idle_notify! would, binding through smithay's state and
adding a can_view that hides it from restricted clients. Everything
else about idle notification is smithay's, unchanged. It adds no
setting, since a sandbox's connection is what decides it.

